course: dictionary
lesson: 12769
title: Words for parts of a house
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a part of a house that you may never have looked at closely. When you meet one, use the words around it as clues. «Swallows built their nests under the eaves of the barn.» Nests are built in safe, sheltered places, and birds fly in and out, so eaves must be a place high up on a building.
explain: The eaves are the lower edges of a roof, where it sticks out past the walls. Try your meaning in the sentence: «Rain dripped from the lower edges of the roof all afternoon» still makes sense, so you have found a good meaning.
explain: Eaves is a plural word, so it means more than one edge. The word «eavesdrop» uses it too. It means to listen secretly, and it comes from standing under the eaves to hear people inside. Words can have a history like this, and knowing it can help you remember them.
fact: The eaves are the lower edges of a roof that stick out beyond the walls of a house.
fact: «Eaves» is a noun, and the plural is «eaves».
fact: Birds such as swallows and house martins often nest under the eaves.
fact: Gutters are fixed along the eaves to catch rain.
fact: «Eavesdrop» means to listen secretly, and it comes from standing under the eaves to hear people inside.
exercise: «Swallows built their nests under the eaves of the barn.» Where did the swallows build their nests? | *Under the eaves of the barn | In the cellar | In the middle of the field | Inside the water tank
exercise: What are the eaves of a house? | *The lower edges of the roof that stick out past the walls | The tall chimney | The front door steps | The walls inside the rooms
exercise: «Rain dripped from the eaves all afternoon.» Which word in this sentence names the part of the roof where the rain came out? | afternoon | dripped | *eaves | rain
test: Tom crept under the window to eavesdrop on the grown-ups. What does eavesdrop mean? | To climb onto the roof | *To listen secretly | To paint a window | To shout loudly for help
test: Mum put a new gutter along the eaves. Why would someone fix a gutter there? | To make the walls look blue | *To catch the rain running off the roof | Birds like to sleep in gutters | To keep the chimney warm
exam: «Icicles hung from the eaves like glass spikes.» Where were the icicles hanging? | Up in the sky above the clouds | On the garden path | Inside the kitchen cupboard | *On the edge of the roof
